You are working on "Stitcher", a browser-only image studio for social media: seamless carousels,
GIFs and videos from frames, and video collages. Read `docs/architecture.md` for how it works and
`docs/roadmap.md` for what's planned before making larger changes.

Core principles:

- Client-side only: no backend, no server APIs, no accounts or authentication.
- All image work happens in the browser (Canvas, Konva, WebCodecs). In-browser models (background
  removal, subject picking, face detection) are fetched as static files on first use and run locally.
- Free, with no watermark and no paywall.

Architecture:

- Vite + React + TypeScript, with the React Compiler.
- TanStack Router with file-based routes under `src/routes/` (the plugin generates
  `src/routeTree.gen.ts`; don't edit it by hand).
- Konva / react-konva for the carousel stage: layers, transforms, snapping and selection.
- The native Canvas API for export: `renderSlide` redraws each slide from the source images.
- JSZip for multi-file exports, gifenc for GIFs, Mediabunny (WebCodecs) for MP4/WebM.

Data and rendering rules:

- The `CarouselDoc` is the source of truth, not the Konva stage. Export never reads the stage:
  `renderSlide` redraws each slide from the doc and the source pixels, at full resolution.
- Docs hold plain, JSON-serializable data. Images are referenced by `imageId` and decoded through
  `lib/image-registry.ts`; never put an `HTMLImageElement` or a `Blob` in a doc.
- Anything drawn on a layer (outline, shadow, blur, look, date stamp) goes through
  `drawLayerContent`, and anything over the whole carousel (light leaks) through `renderSlide` and
  the stage alike, so the editor, the thumbnails and the export match.
- Keep `src/lib` free of React, and keep the geometry and maths DOM-free, so it can be unit-tested
  and shared with a future native app.

State rules:

- Every carousel edit goes through `commit` in `useEditorHistory`. Rapid edits (sliders, drags,
  arrow-key nudges, color pickers) pass a `coalesce` key so they form one undo step.
- Export and view settings (export format, JPEG quality, zoom, overlays) stay out of the doc and
  its undo history.
- New optional fields on `Layer` or `CarouselDoc` must stay optional, so older autosaves still load.

React rules (enforced by the React Compiler lint rules):

- No ref reads or writes during render.
- No synchronous `setState` in effects; derive values during render instead.
- Use `useEffectEvent` for event handlers that window listeners call.

UI/UX rules:

- Keep the UI minimal and tool-focused.
- Styling is plain CSS: shared tokens in `index.css`, the editor shell in `editor.css`. Don't add
  Tailwind or a CSS-in-JS library.
- Each tool is its own route and renders its own `AppNav` with its actions; the tools share one
  shell (control bar, workspace, thumbnail strip, phone dock).
- Every control works on a phone (the dock) as well as on desktop.

Code style:

- Prefer simple functional components and plain functions.
- Keep state local unless it's shared across the stage and the controls.
- Avoid unnecessary libraries or abstractions; heavy dependencies are loaded with `import()` on
  first use, as the models and the HEIC converter are.

Tests:

- Vitest (`npm test`) covers the pure logic in `src/lib` (`*.test.ts` beside each module). Add tests
  for new geometry, parsing or history logic. CI runs them before every deploy.

Naming:

- Source filenames under `src/` use kebab-case (e.g. `carousel-stage.tsx`, `export-carousel.ts`,
  `app-nav.tsx`), not PascalCase or camelCase.
- Single-word names stay lowercase (`main.tsx`, `types.ts`, `index.css`).
- Exported React components are PascalCase in code (e.g. `CarouselStage`); functions and types
  keep camelCase / PascalCase identifiers.
- Import paths match the kebab-case filename (e.g. `./components/carousel-stage`,
  `./lib/export-carousel`).

Do not:

- Do not use Next.js or any server framework.
- Do not add server-side persistence, cloud sync or accounts. Local persistence in the browser
  (IndexedDB autosave) is fine.
- Do not introduce TanStack Query or server-state libraries.
- Do not post to social platforms through their APIs (that needs a server and OAuth).

Goal:

A fast, lightweight, free image studio (carousel stitching, GIF and video from frames, collages)
that matches SCRL's editing power without its paywall, in any browser.
